truly unreachable... unless we mock the comparison? No, the conditions are just boolean logic on the attributes.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 could we make `self.prerelease` an object whose `__bool__` returns different values on successive calls? That's a hack but would work to cover line 203. For example, an object whose `__bool__` alternates. Let's think: \n\n- Condition 1: `not self.prerelease and not other.prerelease` \u2014 calls bool(self.prerelease) and possibly bool(other.prerelease).\n- If self.prerelease.__bool__ returns True first time, condition 1 is False (short-circuit: `not self.prerelease` is False, so `and` short-circuits, other.prerelease not evaluated).\n- Condition 2: `self.prerelease and not other.prerelease` \u2014 bool(self.prerelease) called again. If it returns False now, condition 2 is False (short-circuit).\n- Condition 3: `not self.prerelease and other.prerelease` \u2014 bool(self.prerelease) called third time. If it returns True, condition 3 is False.\n- Condition 4: `self.prerelease and other.prerelease` \u2014 bool called fourth time. If returns False, condition 4 is False \u2192 else branch \u2192 line 203!\n\nSo an object with a `__bool__` that returns True, False, True, False on successive calls would reach line 203. That's a valid way to cover the unreachable branch. But is it \"correct\"? The task says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Line 203 is a missing line. To execute it, we need this trick. Alternatively, we could patch the class... Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the `else` at line 203 is attached differently. Let me re-read the code snippet:\n\n```python\n            elif self.prerelease < other.prerelease:\n       199:                 return -1\n                        else:\n       201:                 return 1\n                    else:\n       203:             raise AssertionError(\"never get here\")\n```\n\nThe indentation: `elif self.prerelease < other.prerelease:` at some indent, `return -1` deeper, `else:` at same level as elif, `return 1` deeper, then `else:` at the level of the outer `elif (self.prerelease and other.prerelease):`, then `raise AssertionError` at the level of `if self.prerelease == other.prerelease:`. So yes:\n\n```python\n        elif (self.prerelease and other.prerelease):\n            if self.prerelease == other.prerelease:\n                return 0\n            elif self.prerelease < other.prerelease:\n                return -1\n            else:\n                return 1\n        else:\n            raise AssertionError(\"never get here\")\n```\n\nSo line 203 is in the outer else, which is unreachable via normal truthiness. To cover it, use the alternating-bool trick or monkeypatch.\n\nActually, another approach: monkeypatch `self.prerelease` to be an object with weird `__bool__`. Or we could directly construct a StrictVersion instance without calling parse (using `StrictVersion.__new__`) and set attributes manually. E.g.:\n\n```python\nv = StrictVersion.__new__(StrictVersion)\nv.version = (1, 0, 0)\nv.prerelease = WeirdBool()\n```\n\nThen compare with another instance whose prerelease is None... wait, but we need both prereleases to be truthy for condition 4 to be evaluated... no wait, we need all four conditions to be False. Let me redo:\n\nCondition 1: `not self.prerelease and not other.prerelease` \u2192 False requires `self.prerelease` truthy (so `not self.prerelease` is False, short-circuit; other.prerelease not evaluated).\nCondition 2: `self.prerelease and not other.prerelease` \u2192 False requires `self.prerelease` falsy (short-circuit).\nCondition 3: `not self.prerelease and other.prerelease` \u2192 False requires `self.prerelease` truthy.\nCondition 4: `self.prerelease and other.prerelease` \u2192 False requires `self.prerelease` falsy.\n\nSo we need self.prerelease to alternate: truthy, falsy, truthy, falsy. And other.prerelease is never evaluated (short-circuit). \n\nSo:\n\n```pyth