sis. Good.\n\nNow, about `test_cmp_string_operand`: if `Version.__cmp__` converts strings, then `v._cmp('1.0')` direct call is needed for lines 170-171. But also, maybe I should test the operator path with strings too (which covers `__cmp__`'s conversion, not `_cmp`'s). The task is about lines 170-203 of `_cmp`, so direct `_cmp` calls are the way. But calling private methods directly is a bit unusual; still, it's the only way to cover those lines if `__cmp__` pre-converts. Let me check get_info first.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider the ansible compat version. I want to recall the actual file. Searching my memory of ansible/lib/ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py:\n\n```python\n# This code is part of Ansible, but is an extracted portion of the CPython standard library distutils module...\n# ...\n# The distutils module originally lived in... \n\nfrom __future__ import (absolute_import, division, print_function)\n__metaclass__ = type\n\n__all__ = ('Version', 'StrictVersion', 'LooseVersion')\n\nimport re\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.six import string_types\n\n\nclass Version:\n    \"\"\"Abstract base class for version numbering classes.  Just provides\n    __cmp__ and __hash__ implemented in terms of _cmp.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__ (self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __str__ (self):\n        return str(self.vstring)\n\n    def __cmp__ (self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, string_types):\n            other = self.__class__(other)\n        return self._cmp(other)\n\n    def __hash__ (self):\n        raise TypeError('unhashable type: %r' % self.__class__.__name__)\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        c = self.__cmp__(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c == 0\n\n    def __ne__(self, other):\n        c = self.__cmp__(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n      